NNAAMMEE
     nncchheecckk__ffffss, nncchheecckk — generate names from inode-numbers

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     nncchheecckk__ffffss [--aammss] [--ff _f_o_r_m_a_t] [--ii _n_u_m_b_e_r _._._.] _f_i_l_e_s_y_s_t_e_m

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     nncchheecckk__ffffss generates a list of filenames and inode numbers for the given
     file system.  Names of directories are followed by a ‘.’.  nncchheecckk__ffffss may
     be invoked more simply as nncchheecckk, with no change in behaviour.

     The options are as follows:

     --aa      Print the file names ‘.’ and ‘..’, which are ordinarily skipped.

     --ff _f_o_r_m_a_t
             Use a custom output format when printing inode information.
             Characters from _f_o_r_m_a_t are printed for each inode with the fol‐
             lowing escape sequences:

                   \\aa      Print a ⟨bell⟩ character.
                   \\bb      Print a ⟨backspace⟩ character.
                   \\ee      Print an ⟨escape⟩ character.
                   \\ff      Print a ⟨form-feed⟩ character.
                   \\II      Print the inode number.
                   \\nn      Print a ⟨new-line⟩ character.
                   \\PP      Print the pathname to the file using the current
                           inode number.
                   \\rr      Print a ⟨carriage return⟩ character.
                   \\tt      Print a ⟨tab⟩ character.
                   \\vv      Print a ⟨vertical tab⟩ character.
                   \\''      Print a ⟨single quote⟩ character.
                   \\\\      Print a backslash character.
                   \\00      Print a NUL character.

             All other characters, when prefixed by a backslash, simply print
             the character following the backslash.  The device name is not
             printed on the first line of output when the --ff option is speci‐
             fied.

     --ii _n_u_m_b_e_r _._._.
             Report only those files whose inode numbers are as listed.

     --mm      Give more verbose information on inodes.

     --ss      Report only special files and files with set-user-ID or set-
             group-ID set.  This is meant to find hidden violations of secu‐
             rity policies.

     The report is not sorted.

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     sort(1), fsck(8), fsdb(8)

HHIISSTTOORRYY
     An nncchheecckk command appeared in Version 6 AT&T UNIX.  The nncchheecckk__ffffss com‐
     mand was designed to be similar in functionality to the corresponding
     command in SunOS 4.1.3.

